<details class="faq-item"><summary>Is freeze-dried raw safer than frozen raw?</summary><p>Generally yes, for two reasons: Stella &amp; Chewy''s use high-pressure processing to eliminate pathogens without cooking, and freeze-drying removes the water that bacteria need to multiply. The FDA found meaningfully high contamination rates in raw pet food overall — around 7.7% Salmonella and 16.3% Listeria in their sampling — so HPP plus batch testing is a real advantage. Still handle it like raw meat: wash hands, disinfect bowls, don''t let your dog lick your face afterwards.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Do I have to rehydrate the freeze-dried patties?</summary><p>It''s recommended and we''d do it. Adding warm water improves digestibility, releases the aroma that makes dogs enthusiastic, and adds moisture — which is one of the main benefits of feeding this way. Some dogs happily eat them dry as a crunchy treat, which is fine occasionally, but make sure fresh water is always available.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Is grain-free food safe for my dog?</summary><p>The FDA has investigated a possible link between grain-free, legume-heavy diets and dilated cardiomyopathy. It isn''t fully resolved. Stella &amp; Chewy''s grain-free recipes are marketed as pea-free, lentil-free and potato-free, which addresses the ingredients under scrutiny, and they also offer Wholesome Grains and SuperBlends lines. If your dog is a breed predisposed to DCM, ask your vet which line they''d recommend.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Can I mix it with my current kibble?</summary><p>Yes, and for most households that''s the smartest approach — meal mixers or dinner dust over a good kibble gives you most of the benefit at a much lower cost. Just remember toppers add calories: reduce the kibble portion accordingly, or you''ll gradually put weight on your dog.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Is it suitable for puppies?</summary><p>They make Perfectly Puppy formulas in both patties and mixers, formulated for growth with DHA for brain and eye development. Large and giant-breed puppies are the exception worth flagging — their calcium and phosphorus intake needs careful control during growth to protect developing joints, so check with your vet before choosing any food for a large-breed puppy.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>How long do the treats I make at home keep?</summary><p>Baked cookies last about a week in an airtight container at room temperature, or three months frozen. No-bake bites need refrigerating and last about a week. Frozen pupsicles and stuffed Kongs keep one to two months. Because there are no preservatives, don''t leave homemade treats out in a bowl — bake small batches and freeze the rest.</p></details>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Stella &amp; Chewy''s earns its reputation. The founding story is real, the high-pressure processing they adopted back in 2007 addresses the single biggest legitimate objection to raw feeding, the meat inclusion is genuinely high, and offering both grain-free and wholesome grains lines is a sensible response to an unresolved question rather than a marketing dodge.</p>
<p>The honest caveats are cost, the fact that raw handling hygiene still matters even with HPP, and that no food is right for every dog — particularly those with existing health conditions or large-breed puppies.</p>
<p>If you''re curious but not ready to spend $40 a bag, start with a <strong>1 oz bag of Meal Mixers or a pot of Dinner Dust</strong> over the food you already buy. You''ll know within a week whether your dog is interested, and you''ll have spent very little finding out. Then go and make them a batch of bone broth pupsicles, because that''s the genuinely fun part.</p>
